  "query_preview": "..Today's sensible weather will be mostly sunny with \ntypical afternoon breeziness and temperatures a degree or two warmer \nthan yesterday. It definitely won't feel quite as windy as the past \ncouple of days when your trash can may have tipped over, but still \nbreezy enough to be noticeable in the afternoon with gusts topping \nout in the 20-25 mph range. Mid/high level clouds will start to \nstream in from the south in the late afternoon/early evening \ntimeframe. Otherwise, high temperatures will be near or just a few \ndegrees above normal through Independence Day, topping out in the \nmid/upper 90s to low 100s. While forecast highs through July 4th are \nforecast to be closer to seasonal levels rather than exceptionally \nhot like in years past, still expect it to be warm enough outdoors \nto pose a risk for anybody who may be more sensitive to the heat.\n\nDiving a little bit deeper into the evolution of the upper level \npattern over the next several days, we start to see increasing mid-\nlevel moisture. Precipitable water will rise into the 0.5 to 0.9 \ninch range across southeast Arizona Friday and into the 0.7 to 1.2 \ninch range on Saturday as an area of high pressure tries to organize \nover New Mexico and meander into the Four Corners advecting plenty \nof moisture from mid-level southerly flow into the region. Most, if \nnot all, of the moisture uptick will be in the mid-levels resulting \nin increasing cumulus clouds each afternoon.\n\nThe increasing moisture will support cloud buildups and increasing \npotential for Monsoonal storms again Monday into the middle of next \nweek with chances (10-40%). We will also see temperatures creeping \nup to several degrees above normal with moderate to locally major \nHeatRisk levels expected by beginning of next week.",
